pseudo-Boolean proof version 3.0
% refused-at: parse
% Regression test (wrong verdict): the formula reader silently discarded
% everything after the first `;` on a line.
%
% the development design notes makes the XPB format line-oriented: every production ends
% with optional *spaces* -- which do not include a newline -- and then
% end-of-line. The reader lexed one line, parsed one item and moved on, so the
% second constraint on this file's single line was dropped and the proof was
% checked against a strict subset of the formula. No error, no warning at any
% verbosity. `f 1;` cannot catch it either, because the count it compares
% against is the truncated one.
%
% `check_constraint_end`'s doc comment claimed it checked "that no further data
% is specified after the semicolon" and its body checked only the `;`. That is
% the whole defect: a documented check that was never implemented.
%
% The formula is unsatisfiable over its one variable -- `x1 = 0` falsifies
% `1 x1 >= 1` and `x1 = 1` falsifies `1 ~x1 >= 1` -- and this used to print
% `s VERIFIED SATISFIABLE` at exit 0 under default flags. The same two
% constraints on two lines were correctly refused, which is what made it
% invisible: every corpus formula writes one item per line.
%
% It also defeated the independent formula cross-check. `veripb-second-reader`
% exists to catch "the formula stored is not the formula on disk", the class
% defects 16 and 17 lived in, and it was written to the same shape and
% inherited the same lenience -- so `--kernel-verify` reported "1 formula
% constraint(s) cross-checked against the file on disk; nothing outside the
% proved fragment" on exactly this input. Both readers are fixed, each on its
% own terms.
f 1;
sol x1;
output NONE;
conclusion SAT : x1;
end pseudo-Boolean proof;
